          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a urinary tract infection, and why are they more common in women?",
          "answer": "A urinary tract infection (UTI) is a bacterial infection of the urinary system. Most involve the bladder and urethra, the tube that carries urine out of the body. This lower tract infection is also called cystitis. The organism *Escherichia coli* causes most of them. If an infection reaches the kidney, that is called pyelonephritis. It is a more serious illness, not just a worse version of a bladder infection. (Source: General clinical reference, \"Urinary tract infection.\")\n\nThe guidance lists several things that make UTIs more likely. Female anatomy is one of them, specifically a shorter urethra. Others include sexual activity, some contraceptives (including spermicides and diaphragms), changes in vaginal bacteria after menopause, incomplete bladder emptying, catheters, kidney stones, diabetes, and previous infections. In men, infections are less common and are more often linked to an enlarged prostate or another structural cause. (Source: General clinical reference, \"Urinary tract infection.\")\n\nThe sources I searched did not explain the reasons for the shorter female urethra in any detail, so I can't say more about the mechanism from them.\n\nIf you have urinary symptoms, a clinician can assess them. Seek urgent care right away if you have fever with chills, back or flank pain, confusion, or feel very unwell, since these can signal a more serious infection. (Source: Sepsis guidance, \"Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection.\")\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
